Cotton Sanitary Pads vs Regular Sanitary Pads: What Should You Know?

Choosing the right sanitary pad can sometimes feel confusing because there are many different options available. You may come across terms such as cotton pads, regular pads, ultra-thin pads, overnight pads and heavy-flow pads. Understanding the differences can help you choose a product that matches your personal needs.

Cotton sanitary pads are often preferred by people who like the feel of a soft cotton-based surface. Cotton is a natural fibre and can be used in different parts of menstrual products. However, sanitary pads are generally made with multiple layers, so a product described as a cotton pad may still contain other materials as part of its construction.

Regular sanitary pads can also have different designs, absorbency levels and surface materials. Therefore, it is not always useful to decide which product is “better” based on the name alone. Instead, consider what works best for your body and lifestyle.

If comfort is your priority, pay attention to the material of the layer that comes into contact with your skin. You can also consider the overall softness, fit and flexibility of the pad.

Absorbency is another important factor. Someone with a heavier flow may need a product with greater absorbency and coverage, while a person with lighter flow may prefer a regular option. Your requirements can also change from one day of your period to another.

Length and shape are worth considering too. Longer pads can provide additional coverage and may be useful during heavier-flow days or overnight. Wings can help keep the pad positioned securely on underwear.

Cotton sanitary pads may be a good option for people who prefer a cotton-based surface, but there is no single sanitary pad that is perfect for everyone. Personal comfort, flow, size, absorbency and fit all matter.

When choosing your next sanitary pad, look beyond the packaging and understand the actual product features. A little research can help you find a product that fits naturally into your period-care routine.

